<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="b7256dcd" lines="18-32">
<description>
Transfer each colored marker to its adjacent neutral mask component, then erase the markers.

Input structure
- The background is the most common color.
- The neutral mask color is the unique most frequent foreground color.
- Other foreground colors are marker pixels that may touch neutral 4-connected mask components.
Rule
- Remove every marker pixel by recoloring it to the background.
- For each 4-connected neutral mask component, find marker colors touching it by edge adjacency.
- If a component touches one marker color, recolor the whole neutral component to that marker color.
- If a component touches no marker color, leave it neutral.
Key insight
- Marker pixels label neighboring neutral regions but are not part of the final colored objects.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="9344f635" lines="15-30">
<description>
Extend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.
- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.

Rule:
- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.
- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.
- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.

Key insight:
- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="dce56571" lines="8-23">
<description>
Straighten the single foreground mask into a centered horizontal segment of the same color and length.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- All non-background pixels share one foreground color.
- The number of foreground pixels fits in one centered row of the grid.

Rule:
- Erase the original foreground mask.
- Count its pixels, keep its color, and paint that many pixels as a horizontal segment.
- Place the segment on the middle row with equal background margin on the left and right.

Key insight:
- The original foreground shape is irrelevant except for its color and area; the output is the area normalized into the grid's central horizontal line.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="97d7923e" lines="81-94">
<description>
Recolor vertical bars by legend-driven rank selection.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Top-left: monochrome blocks touching row 0; each has color K and height k.
- Main grid: vertical bars capped top and bottom by a single color, with solid-color interiors.

Rule:
- For each legend entry (K, k), find vertical bars capped by K.
- Rank bars by interior length (longest = rank 1).
- Recolor the rank-k bar's interior to K.

Key insight: Legend height is a rank index into length-sorted bars, not a literal size.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="c3fa4749" lines="79-96">
<description>
Recolor each side-marked multicolor object embedded in a monochrome panel to its marker color.

Input structure:
- Same-color 4-connected components form monochrome panel regions.
- The bounding box of a panel may contain 4-connected non-panel-color objects.
- A target object touches exactly one side of its panel bbox, and all of its
  pixels on that side have one marker color.

Rule:
- For every target object, fill any panel-colored holes enclosed by the object.
- Recolor the object and filled holes to the side marker color.
- Leave all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
- A multicolor object's single contact side identifies both which object should
  be normalized and which color should replace it.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="e8686506" lines="172-185">
<description>
Assemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.
- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.

Rule:
- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.
- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.
- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.

Key insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="17829a00" lines="21-36">
<description>
Move each colored object vertically until it touches the matching top or bottom border bar.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- The top row is a full-width bar in one foreground color and the bottom row is a full-width bar in another.
- All other foreground pixels are 8-connected monochrome objects whose color matches one of those bars.

Rule:
- Top-bar-colored objects move upward so their bbox top is row 1.
- Bottom-bar-colored objects move downward so their bbox bottom is the row above the bottom bar.
- A downward-moving one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source becomes the continuous vertical segment swept between them.

Key insight:
The horizontal bars are destination anchors: color determines which border an object slides toward, while columns and object shape are otherwise preserved.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="a09f6c25" lines="33-50">
<description>
Recolor each non-singleton red object by its symmetry axis and erase isolated red pixels.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color and red 4-connected foreground objects.
- Every non-singleton red object has exactly one reflection symmetry category:
  horizontal, vertical, or diagonal.
- Single red pixels are noise.

Rule:
- Start from an all-background output of the same shape.
- Recolor horizontally symmetric red objects blue, vertically symmetric objects
  green, and diagonally symmetric objects pink.
- Leave isolated red pixels as background.

Key insight: The target color is not tied to object position or size; it is a
label for the object's reflection symmetry.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="758abdf0" lines="54-69">
<description>
Advance short marker bars away from the full edge bar, turning completed two-pixel markers into edge-color marks at the opposite side.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- One non-background color forms a complete one-cell-thick bar on exactly one outer edge.
- The other foreground color appears only in the first two cells inward from that bar, lane by lane.

Rule
- In each lane perpendicular to the edge bar, a one-pixel marker adjacent to the bar grows one more cell inward.
- A two-pixel marker in those first two inward cells is cleared back to background.
- The cleared two-pixel marker is replaced by a two-pixel mark of the edge-bar color at the opposite side of the same lane.

Key insight
- The full edge bar defines both the orientation and the direction of the local two-step marker cycle.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="c4d067a0" lines="9-23">
<description>
Complete a grid of colored blocks to match a minimap legend in the top-left corner.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Top-left corner: a small sprite of single colored pixels forming a minimap pattern on a regular lattice.
- Main area: uniformly colored square blocks (NxN) arranged on a regular grid; some positions are empty (bg).

Rule:
- The legend pixels define a color pattern; each pixel's lattice position maps to a block position in the main grid via a fixed scale factor and offset.
- Existing colored blocks confirm the scale/offset alignment.
- For every legend pixel whose corresponding grid position is empty, place a new block of that color.

Key insight: The top-left pixel pattern is a scaled-down minimap of the block layout; the mapping is inferred from blocks already present.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="f28a3cbb" lines="42-50">
<description>
Compress each colored ray pixel inward until it touches that color's 3x3 anchor block.
Input structure
- A uniform background grid with non-background colors.
- Each foreground color contains one solid 3x3 anchor block; all other pixels of that color lie on straight 8-neighbor rays aimed at that block.
Rule
- Keep anchor blocks fixed, erase loose foreground pixels, and for each ray pack its pixels into consecutive cells adjacent to the anchor along the same ray.
Key insight
- The scattered pixels are displaced pixels on visible row, column, or diagonal sightlines back to their same-colored 3x3 block.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="66ac4c3b" lines="57-72">
<description>
Complete the sparse-color reflected copy of the foreground motif across its matching marker row or column.

Input structure
- The grid has one background color and two foreground colors.
- One foreground color appears only as a sparse marker row or column.
- The other foreground color contains the same sparse marker positions on a parallel row or column, plus the motif on the far side of the sparse markers.

Rule
- Keep the original grid unchanged.
- Reflect every motif pixel of the larger foreground color across the two parallel marker lines.
- Paint the reflected motif pixels with the sparse marker color.

Key insight
- The two equal marker rows or columns are paired landmarks: the sparse-color marker line borders the source motif, and the matching source-color marker line anchors the reflected copy.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="a25697e4" lines="9-23">
<description>
Dock movable tools into border-anchored frames by filling cavities.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Border-touching components cluster into anchor frames with interior bg cavities.
- Non-border 2-color components are movable tools.

Rule:
- Match each tool to a frame whose cavity size equals one of its color-region counts.

